const title = "GE Dishwasher Not Draining: Causes + Fix — Same Day";
const description = "GE dishwasher won't drain or showing C2? The top causes are the disposal knockout plug, a clogged filter or hose, and the drain pump. Safe checks and when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my GE dishwasher not draining?",
    a: "On GE dishwashers the standing water almost always traces to one of four things, in order of how often we see them: a garbage-disposal knockout plug left in after a new disposal install, a clogged filter or drain hose, a blocked air gap at the sink, or a failed drain pump. The first three are homeowner-checkable in a few minutes. The pump is the tech job. Start with the disposal, because it is the single most common and most overlooked cause."
  },
  {
    q: "What does the C2 code mean on a GE dishwasher?",
    a: "C2 means the dishwasher tried to pump out but couldn't finish draining within its maximum time, roughly seven minutes. It is a drain-timeout code, not a diagnosis of which part failed. The cause is the same list as any GE drain problem: a blockage in the disposal, filter, hose, or air gap, or a weak drain pump. Clear the blockages first; if C2 returns with everything clear, the pump is the likely culprit."
  },
  {
    q: "I just installed a garbage disposal and now the dishwasher won't drain.",
    a: "That is the classic one. New garbage disposals ship with a solid knockout plug sealing the dishwasher drain port. If the installer didn't punch it out, the dishwasher has nowhere to drain and water sits in the tub. The fix is to knock out that plug at the disposal inlet and clear the piece so it doesn't wash back into the pump. If you're comfortable under the sink it's a quick DIY; if not, we handle it on a visit."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I clear a GE dishwasher that won't drain?",
    a: "Safe, in order: scoop the standing water out, then remove and rinse the filter at the bottom of the tub, check the drain hose behind the unit for a kink or clog, run the garbage disposal for a few seconds (a full disposal blocks the shared drain), and clean the air gap on the sink if you have one. Re-run a rinse cycle. If water still stands after all that, the drain pump or control needs a tech."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a GE dishwasher drain pump worth replacing?",
    a: "Usually yes. A drain pump replacement is a mid-range repair and far cheaper than a new dishwasher, so on any unit under roughly ten years old it's an easy call. We confirm the pump is actually the failed part first, because a blockage upstream can mimic a dead pump, and quote before any work. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

